A||||13||fiero56||diamond spoke wheels...sandblasting?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||11-09-2000||10:42 PM||||Still trying to get the paint/cc off diamond spoke wheel. I have tried, time after time with stripper, and can't get the paint on the spokes to cleanly come off. Is it safe for the wheels to be sandblasted, or is it too harsh. I only ask about sandblasting, because I know someone who could do it for me.<P>My only other option I see at the time is buying a gallon or two of stripper, and soaking the wheel overnight. This wheel doesn't belong to me, and I need to re-finish it pretty soon.||||reg||5
Z||000001||2xsess||11-09-2000||11:18 PM||||I'd suggest checking into media blasting, I think it would be as harsh on the metal.<P>Stripper really is a messy job  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||11-10-2000||12:12 AM||||I used a small wooden stick and Permatex spray-on gasket remover. took me about 15 minutes to do a 4 wheels.||||reg||3
Z||000003||webbee||11-10-2000||12:24 AM||||Have you tried lacquer thinner? It seems to cut almost everything. <P>Walnut shells or glass beads might also work without marring the surface, in the blaster.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||11-10-2000||10:45 AM||||I haven't tried the gasket remover. Probably will, the next time.<BR>I used some "gel" paint remover from Home Depot (think it  was called Kleen Strip, or some such), when I did my first set of wheels. Seems like I only needed a couple of quarts.<BR>Was really nasty. Took about three attempts to get all the paint off. <P>Raydar<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||11-14-2000||06:55 PM||||I will look around for the permatex spray on gasket remover. I have been using spray on aircraft stipper, and its not doing the job. Hope this works.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-14-2000||07:55 PM||||One thing that helps with stripper is to put on the stripper then shove the item into a plastic trash bag. (squeaz out all the air you can.) that helps keep the stripper from drying out. let it sit a few hours, even over night with some. preferably someplace about 70-80F.<P>You'd definatly want walnut shells or glass bead blasting not sand if you do that. You also want someone whose done this a few times or you'll need to do allot of polishing.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||zeusrekning||11-14-2000||08:07 PM||||Ogre, mAn You aRe KOOl. I Did The Bag thinG<BR>BUt wHN I sucKED ALL THr air OUT iGet a Brain rush <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> I feel goey.<BR>Nah really .DON'T SUCK THE AIR OUT OF THE BAG.ok <BR>Glass bead would work great. Do it at work all the time. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||11-14-2000||08:51 PM||||I dont know if they use acrylic based or lacquer based c/c on wheels, but another remover you could try is (brand is either dupont or PPG) #3812 Enamel Reducer.  That solvent is what we use to prep the cars before we paint them.<P>You may also be able to just work at it hard enough and buff it off with coarse compound.<P>------------------<BR>"The fish who keeps on swimming is the first to chill upstream"||||reg||1
Z||000009||2xsess||11-14-2000||09:17 PM||||#3812 is a dupont product. I don't know if it will help get the paint off though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||11-14-2000||09:57 PM||||I've tried at least three different brands of paint stripper, Permatex gasket remover, aircraft remover, and sandblasting. I had several wheels from different cars (all Fieros) and it seems like some strip easier than others. Some of them looked great after one or two applications, but some of them seemed impossible to strip. I gave up on one of them and just found another one to do. Abrasive blasting doesn't work as well as you might think. Plastic media or walnut shells are too soft/smooth to cut the clearcoat, and sand is too harsh. Even with sand I had to really work at some spots, but it really erodes the aluminum. If you want the wheels to shine again, sandblasting is out of the question. The ones I blasted got painted with metalic silver urethane enamel and they look decent.<P>I almost forgot, mine were the hi-tech wheels. You could probably tape off the rim of the diamond spoke wheels and sandblast the centers if you plan on repainting the centers.<BR>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||11-14-2000||10:53 PM||||Never thought about masking the exposed aluminum "lip", and then blasting the spokes. The c/c isn't the problem, it bubbles off pretty well. The paint is what is giving me a really big problem, it just won't do anything. Any thoughts positive/negative about sand blasting the spokes, while the aluminum lip is masked off? What should be used to protect the lip?||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||11-15-2000||03:51 PM||||Duct tape or a double layer of masking tape will work fine to protect the rim. If the clearcoat is already off, a really fine sand like 00 or 000 should take the color off easily, and won't damage the aluminum.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||11-15-2000||05:23 PM||||Why are you trying to get ALL the powder coating off? Unless your trying to polish the spokes, stripping the wheels clean isn't necessary. All you really need to do is scuff the powder coating and paint them. <P>Just to make sure the paint sticks well, I would use a very light bead blasting (as mentioned before) and pay special attention to the corners of the spokes. Then, just prep them and shoot them.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
